      "story": "Evlogi and Hristo Georgiev were born in Karlovo. As young men they joined their\nmaternal uncles, the Puliev family, who were merchants in Romania. There the brothers\nbuilt up a large trading and banking business, while also supporting many institutions\nand paying for the education of numerous young people. In 1896 Evlogi donated 800,000\ngold leva and a plot of land in central Sofia for the building of the future university.\nHe died a year later. In his will he left 6 million leva for a higher school in Bulgaria\nand also acknowledged the contribution of his brother Hristo, who had died back in 1872\nat just 48. His relatives expected a large inheritance, but Evlogi left them only a few\nthousand leva and gave almost everything else to churches, schools, hospitals and\nuniversities. The two brothers are buried in Bellu Cemetery in Bucharest, and their tomb\nhas been declared an architectural monument by the Romanian state.\n\nThe road to the building was not an easy one. In 1906 an international design\ncompetition was won by the French architect Henri Bréasson, but construction never\nbegan. Later, the board managing the donation commissioned the architect Yordan Milanov\nto rework the design, despite objections from the university and from Bréasson himself.\nThe foundation stone was laid on 30 June 1924, and the building was solemnly consecrated\non 16 December 1934. Evlogi had asked for two marble statues to be erected in the\nschool's courtyard in memory of the brothers. Today they stand before the university's\ndoors, but they were cast in bronze. The two seated figures are the work of the sculptor\nKiril Shivarov.",
